{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"A SECOND, distinct mechanism producing the same operator-visible symptom — recorded here because it was found in the same session and a fix for one will not fix the other. It may warrant its own ticket; I am not splitting it yet because I am not certain it is a defect rather than documented eventual consistency.\n\n**Observed 2026-09-05:** the local store read lags MCP writes by roughly **90 seconds**. Concretely, an agent called `edit_ticket` to tick five acceptance criteria, got `ok` back, and launched the close seconds later — the close's structural precheck refused with all five boxes reported UNTICKED. The identical read a short wait later showed 5/5 ticked, and the close then passed.\n\nSo a caller that does the right thing — write, confirm success, proceed — gets a refusal describing state that is already false by the time they read it.\n\n**Why I am not immediately calling this a bug.** The store is git-backed with auto-commit and auto-push to `sync.remote`, so an MCP-side write reaching a local CLI reader necessarily involves a push and a fetch. Some propagation delay is inherent to that design, and `docs/concurrency.md` is explicit that the store shares every write immediately in the sense of pushing, not in the sense of every reader seeing it instantly. So this may be correct-by-design and merely under-documented.\n\n**What makes it worth recording anyway.** The failure mode is indistinguishable from a real one. A precheck that says \"these criteria are unticked\" when they were ticked ninety seconds ago is telling the agent something false about their own work, and the natural responses — re-edit, re-verify, or conclude the edit silently failed — are all wrong. It is the same shape as the stale-clone defect above: a message that points at the caller's work when the actual condition is the reader's freshness.\n\n**Two things a fix could take:** a read-after-write barrier on the close path (fetch, or wait for the write's own push to land, before the precheck reads), or a precheck message that distinguishes \"unticked\" from \"unticked as of a snapshot taken at T, which may predate your edit\". The second is cheaper and would have been enough here — the agent lost a close cycle purely to an unqualified claim.\n\n**Practical guidance in the meantime**, which I am now applying myself: after an `edit_ticket` that changes anything a close precheck reads, confirm the change is visible in a fresh read before launching the close, rather than trusting the write's own `ok`. That is the same discipline the completion verifier already demands for evidence comments — write, confirm it landed, then close."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788621678975795765,"uuid":"60f676a9-89b5-4a61-ab85-955b3f4c8ce9"}